# 7. Application Shell Architecture

GrowFlow uses one common shell architecture.

```text
App
│
├── Global Top Navigation
├── Authentication-aware context
├── Workplace Selector
├── Sidebar / Navigation Drawer
└── Main Content Area
```

The shell is shared, while the navigation/content within the shell varies according to the current experience.

---

# 8. Global Top Navigation

Phase 02 established one unified top navigation across the entire website.

The architecture therefore uses one global top-nav system rather than separate Student, Mentor and Admin navbars.

Conceptually:

```text
<GlobalTopNav>
    <GrowFlowLogo />
    <PublicNavigation />
    <WorkplaceSelector />
    <GlobalSearch />
    <Notifications />
    <Profile />
    <AuthActions />
</GlobalTopNav>
```

Visibility is derived from application state.

The global navigation remains consistent across public and authenticated surfaces.

---

# 9. Workplace Selector Architecture

The Workplace selector is a first-class application component.

It represents:

```text
BUILD
SUPERVISE
GOVERN
```

The selector must not independently decide authorization.

Instead:

```text
Authoritative authorization context
        ↓
Available workplaces
        ↓
Workplace UI
```

The backend remains authoritative for actual access.

Logged-in users see workplaces they are authorized to access.

Logged-out users can be presented with the workplace choices defined by Phase 02, with each choice leading to the corresponding authentication/protected entry.

---

# 10. Authentication Architecture

Authentication is centralized.

Conceptually:

```text
AuthProvider
    │
    ├── session
    ├── user
    ├── authentication status
    └── authentication lifecycle
```

The frontend should not unnecessarily maintain independent:

```text
StudentAuthProvider
MentorAuthProvider
AdminAuthProvider
```

Authentication establishes identity.

Authorization determines access.

---

# 11. Authentication vs Authorization

Authentication answers:

> Who are you?

Authorization answers:

> What are you allowed to access and do?

GrowFlow follows:

```text
Supabase Auth
      ↓
Authenticated identity
      ↓
GrowFlow authorization
      ↓
Role/workplace permissions
      ↓
Route + UI access
```

---

# 12. Route Protection

Route protection has multiple conceptual levels.

## Level 1 — Authentication

```text
Is the user authenticated?
```

If not, redirect to the appropriate authentication flow.

## Level 2 — Workplace Authorization

```text
Can this identity access BUILD / SUPERVISE / GOVERN?
```

## Level 3 — Resource Authorization

```text
Can this user access this specific project/group/student/resource?
```

## Level 4 — Action Authorization

```text
Can this user perform this specific action?
```

The backend enforces the actual authorization boundary at every relevant level.

---

# 13. Frontend Permission Model

Frontend authorization should be normalized around concepts such as:

```text
authorization
├── workplaces
├── roles
├── permissions
└── resource capabilities
```

Example permissions:

```text
project.read
project.update
task.create
task.update
mentor-note.create
investigation.inspect
```

These permissions primarily control:

- button visibility
- navigation
- disabled states
- UX messaging

They are not security boundaries.

---

# 14. Routing Architecture

Routing maps directly to the Phase 02 route hierarchy.

The router is responsible for:

- route resolution
- nested layouts
- authentication guards
- authorization guards
- resource context
- route-level loading
- route-level error handling
- deep links

Routes remain:

- lowercase
- kebab-case
- resource-oriented
- nested where context is meaningful

---

# 15. Nested Layout Architecture

Pages within the same contextual workspace should inherit their parent layout.

Conceptually:

```text
GlobalShell
    ↓
StudentShell
    ↓
ProjectWorkspace
    ↓
CurrentProjectView
```

This prevents every page from rebuilding the shell, project header, and contextual navigation.

---

# 16. Project Workspace Architecture

The Student project workspace is a dedicated architectural boundary.

```text
ProjectWorkspace
│
├── Project Header
├── Project Status
├── Project Context Navigation
└── Project Content
```

It hosts the Phase 02 project views:

```text
overview
assessment
blueprint
tasks
milestones
risks
roadmap
documents
github
activity
ai-mentor
help-requests
mentor-feedback
changes
```

The same contextual project model can support the corresponding Mentor project-instance experience.

