# 17. Server State, UI State and Streaming State

GrowFlow explicitly separates three state categories.

## 17.1 Server State

Data originating from the backend:

- projects
- tasks
- milestones
- risks
- users
- notifications
- AI executions
- documents
- groups

## 17.2 UI State

Local frontend interaction state:

- modal open/closed
- sidebar collapsed/expanded
- selected tab
- filter panel
- temporary interaction state

## 17.3 Streaming State

State associated with active delivery/execution streams:

- AI generation progress
- execution events
- job status updates
- document processing updates

These categories must not be casually merged into one global state object.

---

# 18. Server State Architecture

Server state should be handled through a dedicated data-fetching/cache mechanism.

Conceptually:

```text
Component
    ↓
Domain Query
    ↓
API Layer
    ↓
FastAPI
```

Avoid arbitrary raw requests scattered throughout UI components.

Prefer domain-owned abstractions equivalent to:

```text
useProject(projectId)
useTasks(projectId)
useNotifications()
```

The exact library is intentionally deferred to later technical decisions.

---

# 19. Cache Ownership

Server-state caches should have explicit ownership.

Examples:

```text
Project Query
Task Query
Milestone Query
Risk Query
Notification Query
```

Avoid a giant state object such as:

```text
globalProjectStateEverything
```

Granular ownership makes invalidation and refresh more predictable.

---

# 20. Cache Invalidation

When a task changes:

```text
Task mutation
      ↓
Backend confirms
      ↓
Task state updates/invalidation
      ↓
Affected project representations refresh
```

The frontend should not assume that every related representation is automatically correct merely because one local mutation succeeded.

Backend responses remain authoritative.

---

# 21. Optimistic Updates

Optimistic updates should be selective.

Good candidates:

- simple UI preferences
- low-risk interaction toggles
- non-critical presentation changes

Use caution with:

- project lifecycle transitions
- task completion
- milestone transitions
- risk status
- blueprint approval
- AI operations
- permissions
- destructive actions

For important domain mutations:

```text
User action
   ↓
Backend
   ↓
Confirmed result
   ↓
Frontend state update
```

---

# 22. REST Architecture

Frontend/backend communication remains:

```text
Frontend
   ↓
REST
   ↓
FastAPI
```

The frontend should use a centralized HTTP/API abstraction responsible for:

- base URL
- authentication
- headers
- request serialization
- response parsing
- error normalization
- cancellation
- appropriate retry behavior

---

# 23. Domain API Clients

Avoid a single enormous API module.

Prefer domain boundaries such as:

```text
projects.api
project-definitions.api
project-instances.api
tasks.api
milestones.api
risks.api
documents.api
github.api
notifications.api
ai.api
```

Each domain owns its API interactions.

---

# 24. SSE Architecture

SSE is a transport/delivery mechanism.

It is not canonical application state.

Correct flow:

```text
Persistent backend event/state
          ↓
SSE
          ↓
Frontend receives event
          ↓
Update/refetch relevant representation
          ↓
Backend remains authoritative
```

The frontend must be able to recover if events are missed.

---

# 25. Long-Running AI Execution Architecture

AI execution must survive page navigation.

```text
User starts AI operation
        ↓
Persistent backend execution
        ↓
Execution ID
        ↓
Frontend displays status
        ↓
User can navigate away
        ↓
Backend continues
        ↓
Event / notification
        ↓
User returns to execution/resource
```

The component lifecycle must never determine whether the backend job exists.

---

# 26. AI Execution State

AI executions should expose explicit backend-defined states.

Example:

```text
QUEUED
RUNNING
VALIDATING
QA
COMPLETED
FAILED
CANCELLED
```

The frontend renders these states rather than inventing its own canonical state machine.

---

# 27. AI UI Architecture

AI functionality is treated as a domain.

Conceptually:

```text
AI
├── execution
├── status
├── progress
├── messages
├── artifacts
├── errors
└── quality
```

This supports:

- Student AI Mentor
- Blueprint Generation
- Project Changes
- Mentor AI
- Admin AI Observatory

---

# 28. Agent Visibility Rule

The frontend must not hardcode internal LangGraph/agent implementation details throughout the UI.

Avoid widespread assumptions such as:

```text
if agent === "technology_agent"
```

Instead, backend execution data should expose structured information such as:

```text
agent label
status
duration
output
error
```

The frontend renders the supplied structured representation.

This keeps the internal agent architecture evolvable.

---

# 29. Notifications Architecture

Notifications are a shared domain.

```text
Notification API
      ↓
Notification Query
      ↓
Notification Center
```

Real-time notification flow:

```text
Backend event
      ↓
SSE
      ↓
Notification state update
      ↓
Top-nav indicator
      ↓
Notification Center
```

Notifications can deep-link to canonical resources/routes.

---

# 30. Search Architecture

Global Search is a shared domain.

```text
Global Search
      ↓
Search API
      ↓
Authorization-aware results
```

The frontend should not treat arbitrary locally cached data as the authoritative global search index.

---

# 31. Forms Architecture

Forms follow:

```text
UI Form
   ↓
Client Validation
   ↓
API Submission
   ↓
Server Validation
```

Client validation improves user experience.

Server validation remains authoritative.

---

# 32. Form State Ownership

Temporary form state should normally be locally owned.

Example:

```text
Create Project
```

owns its draft fields until submission.

Only persisted data becomes server state after successful backend confirmation.

Avoid placing every form field into global application state.

---

# 33. Loading-State Architecture

Loading is a first-class UI concern.

Different situations require different states.

### Initial page loading

Skeleton or page-level loading state.

### Partial loading

Section-level skeleton.

### Background refresh

Subtle refresh indicator.

### AI execution

Execution-specific progress UI.

### Navigation loading

Route transition/loading state.

A single generic spinner should not be the universal loading strategy.

---

# 34. Empty-State Architecture

Every major collection should define an intentional empty state.

Examples:

```text
No projects yet
No tasks
No milestones
No risks
No notifications
No help requests
No students
No groups
```

An empty state should explain:

1. What is empty
2. Why it may be empty
3. What the user can do next

---

# 35. Error Architecture

Errors should be categorized.

```text
Authentication Error
Authorization Error
Validation Error
Not Found
Conflict
Network Error
Server Error
AI Error
Processing Error
Unknown Error
```

Avoid reducing every failure to:

> Something went wrong.

Instead, normalize backend/client failures into a predictable frontend error model.

---

# 36. Error Boundaries

Use layered failure containment:

```text
Application Error Boundary
        ↓
Route Error Boundary
        ↓
Feature Error Boundary
        ↓
Component-level handling
```

A failure in one feature, such as an AI panel, should not unnecessarily crash the entire application.

---

# 37. 401 / 403 / 404

These remain distinct:

```text
401 → Authentication required
403 → Authenticated but unauthorized
404 → Resource/route not found
```

This directly preserves the Phase 02 architecture.

---

# 38. Responsive Architecture

GrowFlow should support:

```text
Desktop
Tablet
Mobile
```

using one responsive application rather than separate frontend applications.

Desktop is expected to support the highest information density, particularly for:

- Mentor
- Admin
- complex Student project work

Mobile prioritizes navigation accessibility and focused workflows.

---

# 39. Mobile Navigation

Desktop:

```text
Top Navigation
Sidebar
Content
```

Mobile:

```text
Top Navigation
      ↓
Navigation Drawer
      ↓
Content
```

The navigation model remains consistent; presentation changes by viewport.

---

# 40. Component Architecture

Components should have clear ownership levels.

## Primitive

```text
Button
Input
Badge
```

## Composite

```text
SearchBar
DataTable
ProjectCard
NotificationItem
```

## Domain Component

```text
TaskCard
RiskCard
BlueprintStatus
AIExecutionPanel
```

## Experience Component

```text
StudentDashboard
MentorOverview
AdminObservatory
```

---

# 41. God Component Prevention

Avoid components that simultaneously own:

- fetching
- routing
- task rendering
- milestone rendering
- AI logic
- modal logic
- permissions
- API calls
- SSE handling
- formatting

For example, avoid turning `ProjectWorkspace.tsx` into the entire application.

Prefer:

```text
ProjectWorkspace
├── ProjectHeader
├── ProjectNavigation
├── ProjectStatus
├── ProjectContent
└── ProjectActions
```

with domain logic owned by the appropriate domain/data layers.

