# 64. Testing Architecture

Testing exists at multiple levels.

## Unit Tests

For:

- utilities
- pure transformations
- domain helpers

## Component Tests

For:

- shared components
- domain components
- interaction behavior

## Integration Tests

For:

- API + domain state
- forms
- workflows
- permissions

## End-to-End Tests

For critical journeys:

```text
Student registration
Student project creation
Assessment
Blueprint generation
Task workflow
Mentor supervision
Admin investigation
```

---

# 65. AI Workflow Testing

AI workflows require explicit testing of:

```text
QUEUED
RUNNING
COMPLETED
FAILED
```

and lifecycle events such as:

```text
browser disconnect
page navigation
refresh
reconnection
notification
late completion
```

The UI must recover correctly from these situations.

---

# 66. SSE Failure Recovery

SSE may disconnect.

The frontend must support:

```text
Connected
    ↓
Disconnected
    ↓
Reconnect / retry
    ↓
Reconcile current server state
```

Do not assume that every missed event can be reconstructed purely from local state.

After reconnection, the frontend should be able to reconcile against backend state.

---

# 67. Browser Refresh Resilience

Refreshing the browser must not destroy workflow progress.

Example:

```text
Blueprint generation
        ↓
Browser refresh
        ↓
Frontend reconnects
        ↓
Fetch execution state
        ↓
Determine current status
        ↓
Resume correct UI
```

---

# 68. Network Failure Architecture

The frontend should distinguish:

```text
backend unavailable
network unavailable
request timeout
SSE disconnected
```

Not every failure means the user's workflow or canonical data has been lost.

---

# 69. Notification + Long-Running Work Integration

The previously established persistent job model connects directly to notifications:

```text
AI Job
  ↓
Persistent execution
  ↓
Event
  ↓
Notification
  ↓
Canonical resource/workflow URL
```

The frontend can therefore remain resilient even when the user leaves the current screen.

---

# 70. Document Viewer Architecture

Documents should distinguish:

```text
document metadata
document content
document generation state
document preview
```

This is important for:

- blueprint documents
- README documents
- generated project documentation
- RAG-related documents

Large document content should not automatically be treated as lightweight metadata.

---

# 71. GitHub Architecture

GitHub is a domain integration.

Frontend:

```text
GitHub UI
 ↓
GrowFlow backend
 ↓
GitHub integration
```

The frontend should not directly own GitHub credentials or bypass the backend integration boundary.

---

# 72. Activity Architecture

Activity is a derived representation.

```text
Canonical domain change
      ↓
Backend event/activity record
      ↓
Frontend activity feed
```

The activity feed displays what happened.

It does not define the underlying canonical project state.

---

# 73. Analytics Architecture

Analytics follows:

```text
Canonical backend state
        ↓
Events / Metrics
        ↓
Analytics backend
        ↓
Frontend visualization
```

Frontend charts are representations, not canonical sources of platform metrics.

---

# 74. RAG Frontend Architecture

RAG frontend surfaces may expose:

- processing status
- source documents
- ingestion state
- retrieval diagnostics where authorized

The frontend does not own:

- vector storage
- retrieval truth
- embedding generation
- RAG canonical state

Those remain backend responsibilities.

---

# 75. No Direct Database Access

The frontend must never communicate directly with PostgreSQL.

Canonical architecture:

```text
Browser
 ↓
FastAPI
 ↓
Application Services
 ↓
Database
```

---

# 76. No Direct Agent Execution

The frontend must never directly invoke:

- LangGraph
- agents
- agent tools
- LLM providers

Correct architecture:

```text
Frontend
 ↓
FastAPI
 ↓
Application / Generation Service
 ↓
LangGraph
 ↓
Agents / Tools
```

---

# 77. Application Startup Lifecycle

The frontend boot sequence should conceptually be:

```text
Application starts
      ↓
Load configuration
      ↓
Initialize authentication
      ↓
Resolve session
      ↓
Load authorization/workplace context
      ↓
Initialize application providers
      ↓
Resolve route
      ↓
Render experience
```

This prevents role-aware navigation from rendering before authorization context is available.

---

# 78. Deep-Link Lifecycle

Example:

```text
/student/projects/123/tasks/456
```

while logged out.

Flow:

```text
Deep link
 ↓
Authentication required
 ↓
Student login
 ↓
Authentication
 ↓
Authorization
 ↓
Restore intended route
 ↓
Project authorization
 ↓
Task page
```

This preserves Phase 02 deep-link behavior.

---

# 79. Suspended / Inactive Account

If account state becomes suspended/inactive, the application must not simply continue rendering protected content.

The frontend should distinguish:

```text
authenticated
+
suspended/inactive
```

and render the appropriate system state.

---

# 80. Workplace Resolution

A user may have:

```text
Student only
Mentor only
Admin only
```

or potentially multiple authorized workplaces.

The frontend derives available workplaces from authoritative authorization context.

It must never assume every user has access to every workplace.

