# 94. Final Phase 03 Architecture Rules

The following rules form the proposed Phase 03 freeze set.

## Architecture

1. One frontend application.
2. One shared design language.
3. One global top navigation.
4. Role-specific experiences live beneath the common application shell.
5. Domain-oriented architecture is preferred over page-only architecture.
6. Shared functionality is centralized only when genuinely shared.
7. Role-specific behavior remains role-specific.

## State

8. Backend owns canonical state.
9. Frontend owns representation state.
10. Server state, UI state and streaming state remain separate.
11. Global state is intentionally minimal.
12. URL owns shareable/navigation-relevant view state.
13. Forms own temporary draft state.

## API

14. Frontend communicates with backend through REST/SSE.
15. API communication is centralized.
16. Domain API clients own domain interactions.
17. Components do not directly perform arbitrary HTTP requests.
18. Frontend never directly accesses PostgreSQL.
19. Frontend never directly invokes AI providers.

## Authentication

20. Authentication and authorization remain separate.
21. Backend authorization is authoritative.
22. Frontend authorization is UX-oriented.
23. Workplace access comes from authoritative authorization context.
24. Deep links preserve intended destinations.

## AI

25. AI executions are persistent backend jobs.
26. Frontend component lifetime does not determine execution lifetime.
27. SSE is delivery, not truth.
28. Reconnection must reconcile with backend state.
29. AI states are backend-defined.
30. Frontend does not hardcode LangGraph internals.

## Scalability

31. Large collections use server-side pagination/filtering.
32. Heavy features can be lazy-loaded.
33. Large tables/logs/traces can use virtualization.
34. Domain modules have clear ownership.
35. God components and god API files are prohibited.

## Reliability

36. 401, 403 and 404 remain distinct.
37. Loading, empty and error states are first-class.
38. Errors are normalized.
39. Error boundaries isolate failures.
40. Network/SSE failures have recovery paths.

## Security

41. Nothing in frontend is trusted as a security boundary.
42. No secrets are exposed to browser code.
43. AI provider credentials remain backend-only.
44. Resource/action authorization is enforced server-side.

## Maintainability

45. Components consume domain abstractions rather than raw infrastructure.
46. Cross-domain communication follows explicit contracts.
47. Backend/frontend contracts should be strongly typed where practical.
48. Observability is built into the architecture.
49. Accessibility is built into shared primitives.
50. Performance is treated as an engineering requirement.

